CHAPTER IV: THE ECONOMICS OF TOMORROW

The imagined administration shall confront one of the great economic questions of the coming century:

Can technological progress make essential goods and services dramatically more affordable while expanding opportunities for human achievement?

The President and Vice President shall commission a national initiative to investigate this question.

Its economic framework shall draw upon the principles of the Contribution Game.

The Contribution Game shall seek to align individual initiative with shared prosperity.

Entrepreneurs shall be encouraged to create useful businesses. Scientists shall receive opportunities to investigate important problems. Workers shall have pathways to develop valuable skills. Investors shall be encouraged to finance productive innovation. Public institutions shall support the foundations upon which private innovation depends.

The administration shall investigate whether advances in automation, artificial intelligence, clean energy, construction technology, and medicine can reduce the cost of essential services.

It shall evaluate policies designed to expand economic mobility and help citizens participate in the benefits of rising productivity.

It shall test proposals using transparent models, independent evaluation, and real-world evidence.

The ultimate objective shall not be to guarantee that every prediction succeeds.

It shall be to build an economy capable of learning quickly, correcting its failures, and generating increasingly valuable opportunities.

The economic prophecy shall declare:

Let innovation create abundance, let fair institutions widen opportunity, and let prosperity be judged by the lives it enables people to lead.

