CHAPTER V: THE GREAT EDUCATIONAL RENAISSANCE

The administration shall recognize that no national strategy for the future can succeed without a serious commitment to education.

The imagined President and Vice President shall establish education as a central instrument of national development.

Mathematics shall be taught as a language of reasoning and discovery.

Science shall be presented as a disciplined method for understanding the world.

History shall cultivate an understanding of the institutions, achievements, and failures that shaped civilization.

Technology shall enable more students to access high-quality instruction.

Universities shall be encouraged to pursue original research, develop new industries, and educate citizens capable of navigating a rapidly changing economy.

The administration shall investigate new approaches to personalized learning, teacher support, educational assessment, and access to advanced coursework.

It shall measure educational success by demonstrated learning rather than prestige alone.

And it shall reject the idea that a child’s future should be determined by the circumstances into which that child was born.

The educational doctrine of the administration shall be called the Renaissance Mandate:

Every generation must inherit not merely the knowledge of its predecessors, but a greater capacity to discover what its predecessors did not know.

