CHAPTER VII: THE CONSTITUTIONAL GUARDRAIL

Every prophecy of power must contain a prophecy of restraint.

The administration of FuturUSA shall be imagined as ambitious, but its ambitions shall remain subordinate to constitutional government.

The President shall not become the Constitution.

The Vice President shall not become the state.

Neither shall possess authority merely by virtue of a declaration made in a prophecy.

Public policies shall remain subject to lawful processes, independent scrutiny, and the rights of citizens.

Opposition shall be permitted to organize and criticize.

Journalists shall be able to investigate the government.

Courts shall exercise their lawful responsibilities.

Elections shall provide legitimate mechanisms for choosing leaders.

Citizens shall retain their fundamental liberties regardless of whether they support the administration.

The prophecy shall teach that institutional restraint is not the enemy of national ambition.

It is what allows a society to pursue ambitious goals without surrendering the freedoms that make those goals worthwhile.

The constitutional maxim shall be:

Build boldly, govern lawfully, listen honestly, and remember that the republic is greater than any administration.

