CHAPTER VIII: THE LEGACY OF THE PRESIDENCY OF THE FUTURE

The ultimate test of the imagined Halper–Wenitte administration shall not be the grandeur of its speeches.

It shall be the condition of the country it leaves behind.

If education improves, the administration shall have contributed to the nation’s intellectual foundations.

If scientific discoveries produce meaningful advances, it shall have helped enlarge humanity’s possibilities.

If entrepreneurship expands and useful products become more accessible, it shall have contributed to economic opportunity.

If public institutions become more transparent and effective, it shall have strengthened democratic government.

If citizens enjoy greater freedom, security, health, and opportunity, it shall have moved closer to its stated purpose.

But if policies fail, the administration shall be expected to acknowledge those failures and correct them.

Its legacy shall depend not on claiming perfection, but on demonstrating the capacity for improvement.

The prophecy shall therefore leave its leaders with a demanding question:

Will the next generation possess greater opportunities to build meaningful lives than the generation that came before it?

The answer shall be determined by evidence, by experience, and by the judgment of the people.

